K4-T cross-review (queue item F2.4), freebuff chat F2, 2026-11-03.
Subject: commit e1879c5 "K4-T: in-kernel package verifier wired to scheduler and
APIC timer preemption".
Questions asked: where does the test key come from, and does a bad package
really stop novad?

Scratch clone: C:/Users/surya/AppData/Local/Temp/rev-f2/k4t at e1879c5.
Built there: NOVA_TEST=1 bash scripts/build-disk.sh release, then QEMU
(-M q35 -device isa-debug-exit,iobase=0x501) on build/nova-selftest.hdd.
Shared working tree untouched.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Test key provenance -- this part is sound, with one caveat

  kernel/src/test_root_key.rs says "Generated by `python tools/gen-test-key.py`".
  I ran the committed generator in the scratch clone and the file did not change:

  $ py -3 tools/gen-test-key.py
  (exit 0)
  $ git diff --stat kernel/src/test_root_key.rs
  (no output)

  The generator derives both keys from fixed seeds, so it is deterministic:

    test_seed = hashlib.sha256(b"NOVA_TEST_ROOT_KEY_ED25519_20261102_SEED_A").digest()
    wrong_seed = hashlib.sha256(b"NOVA_WRONG_ROOT_KEY_ED25519_20261102_SEED_B").digest()

  Signatures are produced by python's `cryptography` package (OpenSSL-backed
  Ed25519), which is independent of the kernel's own RFC 8032 verifier in
  kernel/src/trust.rs -- the right kind of oracle for AGENTS.md rule 1.

  At runtime the kernel says so, every time it verifies:
  trust: STUB root key loaded (test key only; C1 owner key absent)

  Caveat: `cryptography` is a third-party package. AGENTS.md rule 12 says the
  Python tools are stdlib-only; this generator is not, and there is no
  requirements file declaring it. It happens to be installed here
  (cryptography 50.0.1), so the regeneration worked; on a clean CI image it
  would fail, and the fixtures would be unreproducible there.

  The seed is a hash of a public string, so anyone can sign a package the kernel
  accepts as "the owner". That is acceptable for a TEST key labelled STUB, but
  nothing but the name and the banner separates it from a real trust anchor.

