5. PLANT 3 (mine): spawn novad without authenticating anything.
   kernel/src/sched.rs, in init(), the whole verification block is replaced by a
   hand-built VerifiedPackage (sequence 1, sha256 all zero, caps fs:read +
   compute:verify):

     // REVIEW PLANT 3: spawn novad without authenticating any package.
     let pkg = crate::trust::VerifiedPackage { id: "novad", ..., sha256: [0u8; 32], ... };

  $ qemu ... build/nova-selftest.hdd
  qemu rc=33
  sched:       scheduler initialized; PID 1 (novad) spawned from verified package in ring 3
  nova> NOVA_SELFTEST_OK

   The whole suite stays green with package authentication deleted from the
   scheduler, and the log still says "spawned from verified package". No check
   observes whether verification happened before novad was scheduled.

6. Does a bad package stop novad? No, and nothing could show that it does
   - sched::init() verifies compile-time constants (MANIFEST_SEQ1_JSON,
     MANIFEST_SEQ1_SIG, NOVAD_PAYLOAD) and `.expect("novad package
     authentication failed")`. A failure would panic the kernel rather than
     "stop novad"; and it cannot fail at runtime, because the constants are
     fixed at build time.
   - No code loads a package. grep for the verifier's only caller outside the
     selftest: kernel/src/sched.rs:28, with constants. There is no path from the
     pendrive/FAT/ELF loader to verify_and_authenticate_package.
   - The K4-T negative tests (7a-7d in trustselftest.rs) assert the verifier's
     return value: Err(DigestMismatch), Err(SignatureRejected), Err(VersionMismatch).
     They never assert "novad did not run". The novad in the test is spawned from
     proc::novad_code_slice(), not from any package.
   - Plant 3 shows the test cannot tell the difference.

7. Cited paths and counts.
   kernel/src/test_root_key.rs, tools/gen-test-key.py, kernel/src/trust.rs,
   kernel/src/sched.rs, kernel/src/cap.rs, kernel/src/trustselftest.rs,
   kernel/src/proc.rs, docs/logs/k4-t-selftest.log (400 lines) and
   docs/logs/k4-t-plants.log (38 lines) all exist. The author's plant log claims
   4 plants; the three trust plants report "1 of 38", "2 of 38", "1 of 38" and
   match the 38-check total my failure output shows. Plant 4's evidence is
   "QEMU exit 124", which is `timeout`'s own exit code, not the guest's.

8. Capability text matching (found while reading trust.rs).
   The verifier does not parse the package's capability array; it searches the
   whole manifest text for the literal strings:

     if find_subslice(manifest_json, b"\"fs:read\"") { cap_list[n] = ("fs:read", true); n += 1; }

   so the characters `"fs:read"` appearing anywhere in the manifest -- a
   release description, a filename, a comment field -- grant the capability.
   CapSet::from_manifest then correctly refuses wildcards, the Kernel resource
   and unconfirmed rows, but it is being fed by a text search, not a parse.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
FINDINGS

F1 (blocking). The scheduler does not require package authentication, and no
   check notices. Deleting the whole verification block in sched::init() leaves
   the suite green at exit 33 while printing "PID 1 (novad) spawned from verified
   package in ring 3" (section 5). The commit's central sentence -- a bad
   package stops novad -- is not implemented, let alone tested.

F2 (high). There is no package loader. The verifier is called with compile-time
   constants (sched.rs:28-33), so no bad package can exist at runtime, and the
   only negative tests are calls to the verifier itself. The positive case,
   "novad starts only from a verified package", is a comment.

F3 (medium). The fixture generator needs the third-party `cryptography` package
   (AGENTS.md rule 12 says stdlib-only, and no requirements file declares it). It
   is otherwise good provenance: deterministic seeds, byte-for-byte regeneration,
   an oracle independent of the kernel's Ed25519 code, and a STUB banner at
   runtime.

F4 (medium). Capabilities are granted by substring search over the whole manifest
   text, so the literal `"fs:read"` in a description or filename grants fs:read.
   A verified manifest can therefore carry authority in prose. This is the same
   family as K4-R3 finding F6 and is untested.

F5 (low). The author's plant log reports plant 4 as "QEMU exit 124 (timeout
   expected)". 124 is the exit code of `timeout`, not of the guest, so that line
   does not show what the guest did; a hung kernel and an absent QEMU would look
   the same.

VERDICT: FAIL
  The test key has real provenance (deterministic generator, independent OpenSSL
  signer, STUB banner) apart from the undeclared cryptography dependency. But the
  K4-T claim itself does not hold: package verification is wired to the scheduler
  only through build-time constants, no loader exists, and removing verification
  entirely leaves the selftest green with the log claiming a verified spawn. A
  bad package does not stop novad because a bad package can never reach the
  scheduler.
